@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Accessibility a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Accessibility";
    ds:summary "";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "Focus states: \n\n- decision 1 - read only inputs should get focus. open conversation: should the focus ring have a “muted” version for read only items?\n\nA focus ring/border is for focusable elements, not exclusively interactive ones. There is a distinction:\n\nInteractive – an element users can interact with (button, link, input, etc.).\n\nFocusable – an element that can receive keyboard focus, including read-only inputs and in some cases non-interactive content.\n\nEditable – an element whose value/content users can change.\n\nMost focusable elements are interactive, but being editable is not a requirement for receiving focus. A read-only input is still focusable and allows some interaction, like selecting and copying text. It just can't be edited.\n\n[WCAG](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-visible) also explains the purpose of a focus indicator: “The purpose of this success criterion is to help a person know which element has the keyboard focus.” There is no requirement for the focused element to be editable.\n\n[MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Attributes/readonly), for instance, says the following about the HTML read-only attribute: \"Read-only controls can still function and are still focusable.\"".
